#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "89942534-6b03-52ec-8494-830de5eb8128" :question/math-academy-id "e-12558" :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $x^2 + y^2=z^2$ and the line $x +1=y-1 =\\dfrac{z}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n![](images/b0/b0a31426fa8f1d55dcc4a94fb7b6544cdba753d879a4b95c67b66662ce43a26e.svg)\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}+(t+1)^{2} & =(2t)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1+{t}^{2}+2t+1 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+2 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-1=-2 \\\\ y=-1+1=0 \\\\ z=2(-1)=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,0,-2\\right).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1-1=0 \\\\ y=1+1=2 \\\\ z=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(0,2,2\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,0,-2)$ and $(0,2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198228 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "5524aeab-cc64-5da8-bea3-8cd5dd86ebcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5264" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=8t, \\\\ z=2t\\end{aligned}$ \nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =24 \\\\ (4t)^{2}+(8t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =24 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+64{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ 96{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{2})=-2 \\\\ y=8(-\\frac{1}{2})=-4 \\\\ z=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right).$\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(\\frac{1}{2})=2 \\\\ y=8(\\frac{1}{2})=4 \\\\ z=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198229 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "b1f59935-cd61-5c59-aa8d-cf7ba7f151bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5618" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{16}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1$ and the plane $x-z=3$ onto the $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=3\\,⟹\\,x=z+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{((z+3)+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(z+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{2{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{({z}^{2}+4z+4)+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20daa437-703f-5c1f-bfef-06c613eae5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,6,-4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483620} {:answer/id #uuid "68903205-36b5-5e27-95f6-4876748a4c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2,-4)$ and $(1,2,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483771} {:answer/id #uuid "822195d9-b4ca-5da4-a47f-e77424f2c224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483831} {:answer/id #uuid "9738f5d5-30f2-54f5-b519-04f2dece2bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483875} {:answer/id #uuid "d1da9504-c5b6-53eb-a566-9e371e654451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2,-4)$ and $(1,0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9738f5d5-30f2-54f5-b519-04f2dece2bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483875} :answer-field/id #uuid "88c428fa-eb9a-5abc-94ad-cb031dd35e32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7819f027-d28d-5660-b11c-cd0f95f720fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-70957" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t+1 \\\\ y=2t+2, \\\\ z=t+6\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {((t+1)-1)}^{2}+{((2t+2)-2)}^{2}+{(t+6)}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+12t+36) & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t+36 & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t & =0 \\\\ t(t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)+1=1 \\\\ y=2(0)+2=2 \\\\ z=(0)+6=6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(1,2,6).$\n\n- For $t=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-2)+1=-1 \\\\ y=2(-2)+2=-2 \\\\ z=(-2)+6=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,-2,4).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bfa91e7-ae5d-50b6-a073-b1f80896d1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403442} {:answer/id #uuid "1e88bd92-da6c-54d3-aa02-4b44560aa8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(2,1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403443} {:answer/id #uuid "44d63414-8671-5d77-9ee7-bc14c9183ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403444} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc17341-08ec-5304-938b-b53f6c9990cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,-1)$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403445} {:answer/id #uuid "32211501-cf71-57e9-a95f-ae04c3650a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d63414-8671-5d77-9ee7-bc14c9183ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403444} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a93a4ae-2761-5844-9076-70de06e6a2f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b828b568-be9f-5fe6-8a19-4f466a780e15" :question/math-academy-id "q-71131" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1+t \\\\ y=t, \\\\ z=-t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(1+t)^{2}+6(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(1+2t+{t}^{2})+6{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2+4t+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2}+4t & =0 \\\\ t(9t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{4}{9}$ and $t=0.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{9}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1+(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ y=-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ z=-(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{4}{9},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9}).$\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1+0=1 \\\\ y=0 \\\\ z=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0093477b-d27d-5f57-939a-39d4ab06b81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,\\sqrt{3})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336033} {:answer/id #uuid "4611b6f6-1e89-544e-badf-839b16dd205c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,2,-2\\sqrt{3})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336682} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b33e32-ed92-52b1-8d0b-d33015158fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,0)$ and $(3,2,-2\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337761} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d2918c-ad04-5c71-b7e9-2cb1ab498ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,0)$ and $(1,-1,\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338048} {:answer/id #uuid "fce01e91-6d03-5453-9b41-d772afe81fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2b33e32-ed92-52b1-8d0b-d33015158fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,0)$ and $(3,2,-2\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337761} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f84c3c0-c882-5460-a9ce-2e7b0159c5bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21e54935-c131-53e6-8a93-50cf883ef54c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71950" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+5 \\\\ y=-2t, \\\\ z=2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (2t+5)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3}t)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (4{t}^{2}+20t+25)+4{t}^{2}+12{t}^{2} & =25 \\\\ 20{t}^{2}+20t+25 & =25 \\\\ 20{t}^{2}+20t & =0 \\\\ 20t(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(0)+5=5 \\\\ y=-2(0)=0 \\\\ z=2\\sqrt{3}(0)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(5,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(-1)+5=3 \\\\ y=-2(-1)=2 \\\\ z=2\\sqrt{3}(-1)=-2\\sqrt{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(3,2,-2\\sqrt{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(5,0,0)$ and $(3,2,-2\\sqrt{3}).$"}]}
